=begin pod :kind("Type") :subkind("class") :category("basic")

=TITLE class RakuAST::Type::Simple

=SUBTITLE Represent a type without any additional features

    class RakuAST::Type::Simple { }

The C<RakuAST::Type::Simple> class represents a simple Raku type
without any additional features, such as coercion or definedness.

=head2 Object introspection

C<RakuAST::Type::Simple> object is typically created when parsing
Raku Programming Language code.  So most developers will only need to
know how to introspect the objects created.

=head3 is-native

=begin code :preamble<my $type>
say $type.is-native;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-native> method returns C<True> if canonicalized name of the
type consists of only lowercase letters.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-coercive

=begin code :preamble<my $type>
say $type.is-coercive;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-coercive> method returns C<True> if the resolved type is
known to be coercive. Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 name

=begin code :preamble<my $type>
say $type.name;  # RakuAST::Name.from-identifier("foo")
=end code

Returns the C<L<RakuAST::Name>|/type/RakuAST/Name> object of this
C<RakuAST::Type::Simple> object.

=head2 Object creation

There are several ways to create a C<RakuAST::Type::Simple> object.
For simple identifiers it is recommended to use the alternate
C<.from-identifier> and C<.from-identifier-parts> methods to create
a C<RakuAST::Type::Simple> object.  Otherwise one can use the standard
C<.new> method.

=head3 new

=begin code
my $type = RakuAST::Type::Simple.new(
  RakuAST::Name.new(
    RakuAST::Name::Part::Simple.new("Foo"),
    colonpairs => (
      RakuAST::ColonPair::Value.new(
        key   => "ver",
        value => RakuAST::StrLiteral.new("1.1")
      ),
    )
  )
);
dd $type;  # Foo:ver("1.1")
=end code

Creates a C<RakuAST::Type::Simple> object from the given
L<C<RakuAST::Name>|/type/RakuAST/Name> object.

=head3 from-identifier

=begin code
my $type = RakuAST::Type::Simple.from-identifier('foo');
dd $type;  # foo
=end code

Creates a C<RakuAST::Type::Simple> object from the given simple
identifier string (which may B<not> contain any C<::> separators).

=head3 from-identifier-parts

=begin code
my $type = RakuAST::Type::Simple.from-identifier-parts('IO','Path');
dd $name;  # IO::Path
=end code

Creates a C<RakuAST::Type::Simple> object from the given simple
identifier string parts (which may B<not> contain any C<::> separators).

=end pod
